{"reason": "The current baseline trains exclusively on a single 2021 cohort (~170k rows) and omits critical acute violation signals (such as 3-month velocity, open violation density per unit, and multi-year persistence), resulting in LightGBM (0.6963) lagging behind TabularResNet (0.7029) and limiting overall ensemble AP to 0.7046. Expanding training to a pooled multi-cohort dataset (2020 and 2021 cohorts) resolves temporal sampling bias and substantially enriches positive tail observations. In parallel, engineering acute velocity and persistence features while calibrating LightGBM's positive class weighting and wide linear prior weights directly optimizes tail precision for the target ranking metric.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: In extract_point_in_time_features(), engineer short-term acute violation velocity (3-month window Class C count 'c_3m', velocity ratio 'c_3m / (c_1y / 4.0 + 0.1)'), open violation density per unit ('c_open_per_unit'), chronic violation persistence (distinct count of historical years with Class C violations 'c_active_years' and consecutive 2-year Class C flag 'c_chronic_flag'), and hazardous escalation signals ('bc_1y_sum = c_1y + b_1y', 'c_to_b_ratio = c_1y / (b_1y + 1e-4)'). WHY: Recent acute 90-day violation spikes and unresolved open violations per unit are the primary operational indicators triggering emergency municipal re-inspections, while chronic persistence distinguishes systemic landlord neglect from temporary maintenance anomalies. HOW: Compute 3-month indicator masks in df_violations, aggregate year-level distinct counts via group-by inspection year, compute per-unit densities safely using clipped unitsres, and ensure all feature columns are safely cast to float32 with fillna(0.0) without intermediate categorical column type errors. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve extract_point_in_time_features(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str) and compute_target(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str) signatures, returning dataframes keyed by 'bbl' with matching feature columns across train, val, and test.", "model_design": "WHAT: Re-calibrate the LightGBM classifier with positive class weighting (scale_pos_weight=1.5) and update TabularResNet's wide linear branch prior weights to incorporate the newly engineered velocity and persistence features. WHY: Standard symmetric logloss in LightGBM prioritizes global likelihood over tail precision, explaining its AP deficit compared to Focal-Loss TabularResNet; asymmetric class weighting aligns tree split criteria directly with the Average Precision objective. HOW: Configure LGBMClassifier with scale_pos_weight=1.5, min_child_samples=50, and subsample=0.85, and update TabularResNet._init_weights() dictionary to assign positive inductive prior weights to 'c_3m', 'c_open_per_unit', and 'c_active_years' on the wide linear bypass. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve TabularResNet and FocalLossWithLogits classes and interface signatures, accepting in_features and feature_names.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Transition from single-cohort training to a pooled multi-cohort longitudinal training scheme by extracting features and labels for cohort 2020-01-01 (using PLUTO release 19v2 and window [2020-01-01, 2021-01-01)) and concatenating it with cohort 2021-01-01 to form X_train_df. WHY: Training exclusively on 2021 exposes models to an idiosyncratic post-pandemic inspection lull, while doubling training data to ~340k instances across multiple enforcement climates stabilizes neural representations and tree partitions for rare high-risk lots. HOW: Call load_pluto_release('19v2', df_test), extract features at '2020-01-01', compute targets over 2020, concatenate with the 2021 cohort before out-of-fold target encoding, fit StandardScaler on the pooled training set, and fine-tune ensemble probability grid search across [0.0, 1.0]. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Retain strict hold-out validation on cohort 2022-01-01 (PLUTO 21v4), preserve final submission writing to './submission/submission.csv' with columns ['bbl', 'score'], and report 'Final Validation Score'."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current baseline trains exclusively on a single 2021 cohort (~170k rows) and omits critical acute violation signals (such as 3-month velocity, open violation density per unit, and multi-year persistence), resulting in LightGBM (0.6963) lagging behind TabularResNet (0.7029) and limiting overall ensemble AP to 0.7046. Expanding training to a pooled multi-cohort dataset (2020 and 2021 cohorts) resolves temporal sampling bias and substantially enriches positive tail observations. In parallel, engineering acute velocity and persistence features while calibrating LightGBM's positive class weighting and wide linear prior weights directly optimizes tail precision for the target ranking metric.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: In extract_point_in_time_features(), engineer short-term acute violation velocity (3-month window Class C count 'c_3m', velocity ratio 'c_3m / (c_1y / 4.0 + 0.1)'), open violation density per unit ('c_open_per_unit'), chronic violation persistence (distinct count of historical years with Class C violations 'c_active_years' and consecutive 2-year Class C flag 'c_chronic_flag'), and hazardous escalation signals ('bc_1y_sum = c_1y + b_1y', 'c_to_b_ratio = c_1y / (b_1y + 1e-4)'). WHY: Recent acute 90-day violation spikes and unresolved open violations per unit are the primary operational indicators triggering emergency municipal re-inspections, while chronic persistence distinguishes systemic landlord neglect from temporary maintenance anomalies. HOW: Compute 3-month indicator masks in df_violations, aggregate year-level distinct counts via group-by inspection year, compute per-unit densities safely using clipped unitsres, and ensure all feature columns are safely cast to float32 with fillna(0.0) without intermediate categorical column type errors. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve extract_point_in_time_features(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str) and compute_target(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str) signatures, returning dataframes keyed by 'bbl' with matching feature columns across train, val, and test.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Re-calibrate the LightGBM classifier with positive class weighting (scale_pos_weight=1.5) and update TabularResNet's wide linear branch prior weights to incorporate the newly engineered velocity and persistence features. WHY: Standard symmetric logloss in LightGBM prioritizes global likelihood over tail precision, explaining its AP deficit compared to Focal-Loss TabularResNet; asymmetric class weighting aligns tree split criteria directly with the Average Precision objective. HOW: Configure LGBMClassifier with scale_pos_weight=1.5, min_child_samples=50, and subsample=0.85, and update TabularResNet._init_weights() dictionary to assign positive inductive prior weights to 'c_3m', 'c_open_per_unit', and 'c_active_years' on the wide linear bypass. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve TabularResNet and FocalLossWithLogits classes and interface signatures, accepting in_features and feature_names.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Transition from single-cohort training to a pooled multi-cohort longitudinal training scheme by extracting features and labels for cohort 2020-01-01 (using PLUTO release 19v2 and window [2020-01-01, 2021-01-01)) and concatenating it with cohort 2021-01-01 to form X_train_df. WHY: Training exclusively on 2021 exposes models to an idiosyncratic post-pandemic inspection lull, while doubling training data to ~340k instances across multiple enforcement climates stabilizes neural representations and tree partitions for rare high-risk lots. HOW: Call load_pluto_release('19v2', df_test), extract features at '2020-01-01', compute targets over 2020, concatenate with the 2021 cohort before out-of-fold target encoding, fit StandardScaler on the pooled training set, and fine-tune ensemble probability grid search across [0.0, 1.0]. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Retain strict hold-out validation on cohort 2022-01-01 (PLUTO 21v4), preserve final submission writing to './submission/submission.csv' with columns ['bbl', 'score'], and report 'Final Validation Score'."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The baseline model plateaued at 0.6903 AP because it was restricted to a single 12-month training cohort (171k rows), continuously normalized discrete spatial/class identifiers (distorting spatial relationships), and lacked longitudinal acceleration signals. Modifying data processing, model architecture, and training evaluation simultaneously resolves these bottlenecks by pooling multi-year training cohorts, engineering YoY violation acceleration and neighborhood base-rate priors, and learning dedicated entity embeddings in the Tabular Transformer.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Engineer longitudinal violation trajectory features (YoY Class C acceleration ratio and difference between [T-365d, T) and [T-730d, T-365d)), prior community district Class C base rates computed strictly before T, and derive clean zero-indexed integer IDs for discrete attributes (borough, community district, and building class) instead of continuous floats. WHY: Property distress is inherently dynamic and neighborhood-dependent; accelerating violations indicate escalating landlord neglect, while integer categorization prevents pandas categorical dtype errors identified in historical runs. HOW: In extract_features(), compute YoY delta and ratio for both Class C and total violations, aggregate historical CD-level violation rates up to cutoff T, and map borough, community district, and primary building class into safe non-negative integer columns separated from continuous features. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Keep extract_features(entities_df, violations_df, cutoff_timestamp) signature intact and return clean DataFrames with standard column prefixing.", "model_design": "WHAT: Upgrade TabularFeatureInteractionNet to accept both continuous feature vectors and discrete categorical indices, utilizing dedicated nn.Embedding layers for discrete identifiers alongside continuous FeatureTokenizer projections. WHY: Treating discrete community district and building class codes as continuous numbers introduces artificial euclidean distance; dedicated embedding tables allow the transformer self-attention layers to learn realistic spatial clusters and property risk phenotypes. HOW: Modify TabularFeatureInteractionNet.__init__ to instantiate nn.ModuleList with nn.Embedding for borough, CD, and building class (projected to embed_dim=32); in forward(x_cont, x_cat), concatenate categorical embedding tokens, continuous feature tokens, and the CLS token into the transformer sequence before passing through self-attention and the wide highway skip connection. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain build_model(num_cont_features, cat_cardinalities, embed_dim=32, ...) and keep AsymmetricFocalLoss and build_optimizer interface signatures consistent.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement multi-cohort training data pooling by loading and extracting features for the 2020-01-01 cutoff (PLUTO 19v2, target window 2020-2021) and concatenating it with the 2021-01-01 cutoff (PLUTO 20v7), expanding training volume from ~171k to ~340k samples while keeping 2022 validation untouched. WHY: Training on a single annual cohort caused AP saturation by epoch 10; multi-cohort pooling exposes the transformer to diverse multi-year inspection cycles and provides sufficient sample diversity to train categorical embeddings without overfitting. HOW: In the execution pipeline, load PLUTO 19v2 to build the 2020 cohort, concatenate with the 2021 cohort to form train_features, fit StandardScaler strictly on continuous columns, construct TensorDataset with (X_cont, X_cat, y), and execute the training loop with OneCycleLR scheduler and best AP checkpointing. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve validation on the 2022 cutoff evaluated with average_precision_score, and generate submission/submission.csv with columns ['bbl', 'score']."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The baseline model plateaued at 0.6903 AP because it was restricted to a single 12-month training cohort (171k rows), continuously normalized discrete spatial/class identifiers (distorting spatial relationships), and lacked longitudinal acceleration signals. Modifying data processing, model architecture, and training evaluation simultaneously resolves these bottlenecks by pooling multi-year training cohorts, engineering YoY violation acceleration and neighborhood base-rate priors, and learning dedicated entity embeddings in the Tabular Transformer.",
