{"reason": "The current solution underperforms because it isolates tax lots without spatial block/district aggregation, ignores Class B to Class C escalation dynamics, and relies on an under-regularized TabularResNet alongside a single leaf-wise GBDT. Furthermore, training equally on the 2020 cohort without temporal sample weighting introduces distribution shift from pandemic-era inspection disruptions. Modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation resolves these bottlenecks by capturing neighborhood spillover and escalation signals, adding CatBoost's oblivious tree architecture for structural diversity, and stabilizing neural training with learning rate warmup, validation AP checkpointing, and temporal sample weighting.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: In extract_point_in_time_features(), engineer point-in-time block-level violation aggregates (using the 6-digit block key boroid + block over trailing 1-year and 2-year windows, plus lot-to-block violation ratios), Community District (cd) violation density per residential unit, and multi-class escalation dynamics (trailing Class B violation acceleration b_1y / (b_2y - b_1y + 0.5), unresolved Class B to Class C ratio, and open Class C violations per unit). WHY: HPD inspections are spatially routed and adjacent properties on the same block share environmental risk factors, while unresolved Class B hazardous violations directly precede Class C emergency failures. HOW: Extract the 6-digit block key by concatenating borough and 5-digit zero-padded block, compute trailing 1y and 2y sum aggregations of violations per block strictly prior to cutoff, merge back onto entities, compute ratios safely avoiding zero division, and explicitly convert all engineered numeric features using pd.to_numeric(..., errors='coerce').fillna(0.0).astype(np.float32) to avoid the categorical type error observed in past experiments. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve the signatures extract_point_in_time_features(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str) and compute_target(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str), maintaining bbl alignment and identical feature column lists across train, validation, and test cohorts.", "model_design": "WHAT: Introduce CatBoostClassifier (with depth=6, l2_leaf_reg=5.0, iterations=800, early stopping) as an oblivious-tree counterpart to LightGBM, and update TabularResNet's wide linear inductive priors to include block violation intensity and Class B escalation metrics while adding gradient clipping and dropout regularizers. WHY: LightGBM's leaf-wise tree growth can overfit localized sparse counts, whereas CatBoost's symmetric oblivious trees provide orthogonal decision boundaries and superior generalization on continuous ratio features, creating complementary diversity when ensembled with TabularResNet. HOW: Instantiate CatBoostClassifier(iterations=800, learning_rate=0.04, depth=6, eval_metric='PRAUC', random_seed=42, verbose=False) alongside LGBMClassifier, and enhance TabularResNet._init_weights() to include positive priors on block_c_1y and b_to_c_escalation. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain the TabularResNet, TabularResidualBlock, and FocalLossWithLogits class definitions and forward signatures forward(self, x) returning logits.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Apply temporal sample weighting to the pooled training set (assigning weight 0.5 to the COVID-disrupted 2020 cohort and 1.0 to the 2021 cohort), implement a linear learning rate warmup for TabularResNet with strict validation AP checkpoint restoration, and optimize a 3-way blending grid across LightGBM, CatBoost, and TabularResNet predictions. WHY: The 2020 inspection pause distorts raw event rates compared to post-2021 conditions, while unregularized neural training previously degraded after epoch 3, so warmup and early checkpoint selection guarantee the best generalized neural features. HOW: Construct sample_weight array for X_train_df based on cohort year, pass sample weights to LightGBM and CatBoost fit(), train TabularResNet using AdamW with a 1-epoch linear warmup followed by Cosine Annealing, restore the highest hold-out validation AP checkpoint, perform a simplex grid search to determine optimal weights for (w_lgb, w_cat, w_nn), and write final predictions to ./submission/submission.csv. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Validate strictly on cutoff 2022-01-01 against official competition average_precision_score, preserve Final Validation Score: <score>, and ensure ./submission/submission.csv contains columns ['bbl', 'score'] with exactly 171,587 rows."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current solution underperforms because it isolates tax lots without spatial block/district aggregation, ignores Class B to Class C escalation dynamics, and relies on an under-regularized TabularResNet alongside a single leaf-wise GBDT. Furthermore, training equally on the 2020 cohort without temporal sample weighting introduces distribution shift from pandemic-era inspection disruptions. Modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation resolves these bottlenecks by capturing neighborhood spillover and escalation signals, adding CatBoost's oblivious tree architecture for structural diversity, and stabilizing neural training with learning rate warmup, validation AP checkpointing, and temporal sample weighting.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: In extract_point_in_time_features(), engineer point-in-time block-level violation aggregates (using the 6-digit block key boroid + block over trailing 1-year and 2-year windows, plus lot-to-block violation ratios), Community District (cd) violation density per residential unit, and multi-class escalation dynamics (trailing Class B violation acceleration b_1y / (b_2y - b_1y + 0.5), unresolved Class B to Class C ratio, and open Class C violations per unit). WHY: HPD inspections are spatially routed and adjacent properties on the same block share environmental risk factors, while unresolved Class B hazardous violations directly precede Class C emergency failures. HOW: Extract the 6-digit block key by concatenating borough and 5-digit zero-padded block, compute trailing 1y and 2y sum aggregations of violations per block strictly prior to cutoff, merge back onto entities, compute ratios safely avoiding zero division, and explicitly convert all engineered numeric features using pd.to_numeric(..., errors='coerce').fillna(0.0).astype(np.float32) to avoid the categorical type error observed in past experiments. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve the signatures extract_point_in_time_features(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str) and compute_target(df_entities, df_violations, cutoff_str), maintaining bbl alignment and identical feature column lists across train, validation, and test cohorts.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Introduce CatBoostClassifier (with depth=6, l2_leaf_reg=5.0, iterations=800, early stopping) as an oblivious-tree counterpart to LightGBM, and update TabularResNet's wide linear inductive priors to include block violation intensity and Class B escalation metrics while adding gradient clipping and dropout regularizers. WHY: LightGBM's leaf-wise tree growth can overfit localized sparse counts, whereas CatBoost's symmetric oblivious trees provide orthogonal decision boundaries and superior generalization on continuous ratio features, creating complementary diversity when ensembled with TabularResNet. HOW: Instantiate CatBoostClassifier(iterations=800, learning_rate=0.04, depth=6, eval_metric='PRAUC', random_seed=42, verbose=False) alongside LGBMClassifier, and enhance TabularResNet._init_weights() to include positive priors on block_c_1y and b_to_c_escalation. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain the TabularResNet, TabularResidualBlock, and FocalLossWithLogits class definitions and forward signatures forward(self, x) returning logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Apply temporal sample weighting to the pooled training set (assigning weight 0.5 to the COVID-disrupted 2020 cohort and 1.0 to the 2021 cohort), implement a linear learning rate warmup for TabularResNet with strict validation AP checkpoint restoration, and optimize a 3-way blending grid across LightGBM, CatBoost, and TabularResNet predictions. WHY: The 2020 inspection pause distorts raw event rates compared to post-2021 conditions, while unregularized neural training previously degraded after epoch 3, so warmup and early checkpoint selection guarantee the best generalized neural features. HOW: Construct sample_weight array for X_train_df based on cohort year, pass sample weights to LightGBM and CatBoost fit(), train TabularResNet using AdamW with a 1-epoch linear warmup followed by Cosine Annealing, restore the highest hold-out validation AP checkpoint, perform a simplex grid search to determine optimal weights for (w_lgb, w_cat, w_nn), and write final predictions to ./submission/submission.csv. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Validate strictly on cutoff 2022-01-01 against official competition average_precision_score, preserve Final Validation Score: <score>, and ensure ./submission/submission.csv contains columns ['bbl', 'score'] with exactly 171,587 rows."
