}"}

{"reason": "The baseline model plateaued at 0.7068 AP within 3 minutes because of an architectural bottleneck (linear 32-dim continuous feature tokenization and a narrow highway layer), lack of acute heating-season and chronic landlord recidivism features, and optimization misalignment from a purely pointwise focal loss on an imbalanced ranking objective. Modifying all three components addresses the root causes by enriching point-in-time domain representations with chronic recidivism, open violation failure ratios, and 60-day winter heating acceleration, scaling model capacity via Gated Linear Unit (GLU) continuous tokenization and multi-scale pooling, and optimizing directly for Average Precision via a composite ranking-aware margin loss.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Engineer chronic violation recidivism indicators (distinct count of active years with Class C violations over the 3-year lookback, consecutive-year violation flag), violation resolution failure ratios (unresolved open Class C violations divided by total Class C violations, and open total violations divided by all violations), winter heating season acceleration (Class C violation count in the immediate 60-day pre-cutoff window relative to the prior 60-day baseline), and hyper-local tax-block spatial violation base rates using the 6-digit tax block prefix (df['bbl'].str[:6]). WHY: Class C violations reflect immediately hazardous conditions (such as heat and hot water outages) heavily concentrated in chronically neglected buildings where landlords fail to remediate open violations, and because the cutoff date of January 1 occurs in the middle of winter, 60-day pre-cutoff heat season acceleration and tax-block distress priors provide crucial predictive signals. HOW: In extract_features(), compute distinct inspection years per BBL in v_c, derive open violation ratios with safe positive epsilon denominators, compute 60-day window slices in v_c, calculate block-level mean prior rates on df['bbl'].str[:6], and cast all newly engineered columns using pd.to_numeric(..., errors='coerce') to safely avoid pandas categorical dtype assignment errors identified in previous iterations. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve the function signature extract_features(entities_df, violations_df, cutoff_timestamp) and return a clean DataFrame containing 'bbl', discrete categorical columns, and numeric columns prefixed with 'feat_'.", "model_design": "WHAT: Upgrade TabularFeatureInteractionNet by replacing the single linear FeatureTokenizer with a Gated Linear Unit (GLU) continuous tokenizer, increasing embedding dimension from 32 to 64, expanding the wide highway into a 2-layer residual MLP with LayerNorm, and introducing multi-scale pooling that concatenates the CLS token with mean-pooled feature tokens. WHY: Tabular physical attributes and violation counts exhibit sharp non-linear threshold effects that linear tokenizers cannot represent, squashing 45+ features into a single 32-dim linear layer created a representational bottleneck, and combining CLS context with mean-pooled tokens ensures both localized feature signals and global attention reach the classification head. HOW: In FeatureTokenizer, implement a GLU transformation projecting continuous inputs to 2 * embed_dim followed by gated activation (linear * sigmoid(gate)); in TabularFeatureInteractionNet, set embed_dim=64, deepen the wide highway into a two-layer MLP with GELU and LayerNorm, and concatenate cls_out, tokens[:, 1:, :].mean(dim=1), and wide_out before passing into the final classification head. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain the class names FeatureTokenizer, TabularFeatureInteractionNet, and build_model(num_cont_features, cat_cardinalities, embed_dim=64, ...) ensuring compatibility with the training loop.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Transition the training objective from purely pointwise Asymmetric Focal Loss with pos_weight=1.0 to a Composite AP-Ranking Loss that couples positive-reweighted Asymmetric Focal Loss (pos_weight=2.5) with an auxiliary pairwise margin ranking loss computed over mini-batch positive/negative pairs. WHY: The competition evaluation metric is Average Precision (AP), which evaluates rank ordering across all test lots; standard pointwise loss updates samples independently, whereas auxiliary pairwise margin ranking penalizes rank inversions directly whenever a negative lot is scored higher than a positive lot. HOW: Implement CompositeRankingLoss combining AsymmetricFocalLoss(pos_weight=2.5) with F.margin_ranking_loss(pos_logits, neg_logits, target=1.0, margin=0.5) weighted by an auxiliary coefficient of 0.2 on sampled positive-negative lot pairs within each mini-batch; adjust build_loss() to instantiate this composite criterion while preserving the AdamW optimizer, OneCycleLR scheduler, and validation metric tracking. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Retain strict temporal hold-out validation on cohort 2022-01-01 evaluated using average_precision_score, ensure submission/submission.csv is saved with columns ['bbl', 'score'], and print 'Final Validation Score:'."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The baseline model plateaued at 0.7068 AP within 3 minutes because of an architectural bottleneck (linear 32-dim continuous feature tokenization and a narrow highway layer), lack of acute heating-season and chronic landlord recidivism features, and optimization misalignment from a purely pointwise focal loss on an imbalanced ranking objective. Modifying all three components addresses the root causes by enriching point-in-time domain representations with chronic recidivism, open violation failure ratios, and 60-day winter heating acceleration, scaling model capacity via Gated Linear Unit (GLU) continuous tokenization and multi-scale pooling, and optimizing directly for Average Precision via a composite ranking-aware margin loss.",
  "module": [
